      "observed_position": 42,
      "last_observation": {
        "kind": "question",
        "selector": "#questionContainer",
        "key": "diagnostic-43",
        "sequence_position": 43,
        "accepted": true,
        "grade": "Skipped Question",
        "ready": true,
        "next": "#nextButton",
        "remaining_seconds": null,
        "source_url": "https://mathacademy.com/tasks/14111253/diagnostics/453778",
        "observed_at": 1791714021.7862692,
        "deadline_at": null,
        "question_count": null,
        "browser_events": [],
        "html_path": "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14111253/observations/diagnostic-43-dd1c94d2cb4270d2d647.html",
        "screenshot": "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14111253/observations/diagnostic-43-dd1c94d2cb4270d2d647.png",
        "worked_solution": "If $F$ is a vector field and $C$ is a piecewise-smooth, simple, closed curve, then the flux of $F$ across $C$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s,\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ denotes the outward-pointing unit normal vector to $C.$ In this case, we have\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot {n}_{1}\\,\\text{d}s+{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot {n}_{2}\\,\\text{d}s+{\\int }_{{C}_{3}}F\\cdot {n}_{3}\\,\\text{d}s+{\\int }_{{C}_{4}}F\\cdot {n}_{4}\\,\\text{d}s,\n$$\n\nwhere the sides ${C}_{i}$ and unit vectors ${n}_{i}$ are shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14111253/assets/0950c559d5d6df93cf40afd8fdbd07f090f864fa462083bfd496246e7f2bded9.png)\n\nWe compute this integral in two stages. \n\n- First, consider the side ${C}_{1}.$ We have that ${n}_{1}=i,$ and therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot {n}_{1}\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{{C}_{1}}(3x\\,i-2y\\,j)\\cdot i\\,\\text{d}s \\\\  & =3{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}x\\,\\text{d}s.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is a line integral with respect to arc length, which we can evaluate in the usual way. Parameterizing the side ${C}_{1},$ we have\n\n$$\nr(t)=2i+t\\,j,\\,⇒\\,{r}^{′}(t)=j,\\,t\\in [-3,3].\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}x\\,\\text{d}s & =3{\\int }_{-3}^{3}2\\cdot ||{r}^{′}(t)||\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =6{\\int }_{-3}^{3}\\text{d}t \\\\  & =6\\cdot 6 \\\\  & =36.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy symmetry, the flux integral across the side ${C}_{3}$ must be the same. Therefore,\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot {n}_{1}\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{C}_{3}}F\\cdot {n}_{3}\\,\\text{d}s=36.\n$$\n\n- Next, we consider the side ${C}_{2}.$ We have that ${n}_{2}=j,$ and therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot {n}_{2}\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{{C}_{2}}(3x\\,i-2y\\,j)\\cdot j\\,\\text{d}s \\\\  & =-2{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}y\\,\\text{d}s.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is a line integral with respect to arc length, which we can evaluate in the usual way. Parameterizing the side ${C}_{2},$ we have\n\n$$\nr(t)=t\\,i+3j,\\,⇒\\,{r}^{′}(t)=i,\\,t\\in [-2,2].\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-2{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}y\\,\\text{d}s & =-2{\\int }_{-2}^{2}3\\cdot ||{r}^{′}(t)||\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-6{\\int }_{-2}^{2}\\text{d}t \\\\  & =(-6)\\cdot 4 \\\\  & =-24.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy symmetry, the flux integral across the side ${C}_{4}$ must be the same. Therefore,\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot {n}_{2}\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{C}_{4}}F\\cdot {n}_{4}\\,\\text{d}s=-24.\n$$\n\nFinally, then\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s=2\\cdot 36+2\\cdot (-24)=24.\n$$",
        "can_dont_know": false,
        "can_skip": false,
        "question": {
          "dom_id": "questionContainer",
          "name": "Question 43",
          "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14111253/assets/de3e018fa1382ee2250d1b2e256e3b47cafef346852007f0e6afb84342d7cb04.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $F=3x\\,i-2y\\,j$ and a rectangle $C$ with base $4$ and height $6$ centered at $O,$ as shown above. Calculate the flux of $F$ across $C.$\n\n*Hint: You may use the fact that, due to symmetry, the flux is equal across the vertical sides, and similarly for the horizontal sides.*",
          "worked_solution": "If $F$ is a vector field and $C$ is a piecewise-smooth, simple, closed curve, then the flux of $F$ across $C$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s,\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ denotes the outward-pointing unit normal vector to $C.$ In this case, we have\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot {n}_{1}\\,\\text{d}s+{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot {n}_{2}\\,\\text{d}s+{\\int }_{{C}_{3}}F\\cdot {n}_{3}\\,\\text{d}s+{\\int }_{{C}_{4}}F\\cdot {n}_{4}\\,\\text{d}s,\n$$\n\nwhere the sides ${C}_{i}$ and unit vectors ${n}_{i}$ are shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14111253/assets/0950c559d5d6df93cf40afd8fdbd07f090f864fa462083bfd496246e7f2bded9.png)\n\nWe compute this integral in two stages. \n\n- First, consider the side ${C}_{1}.$ We have that ${n}_{1}=i,$ and therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot {n}_{1}\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{{C}_{1}}(3x\\,i-2y\\,j)\\cdot i\\,\\text{d}s \\\\  & =3{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}x\\,\\text{d}s.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is a line integral with respect to arc length, which we can evaluate in the usual way. Parameterizing the side ${C}_{1},$ we have\n\n$$\nr(t)=2i+t\\,j,\\,⇒\\,{r}^{′}(t)=j,\\,t\\in [-3,3].\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}x\\,\\text{d}s & =3{\\int }_{-3}^{3}2\\cdot ||{r}^{′}(t)||\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =6{\\int }_{-3}^{3}\\text{d}t \\\\  & =6\\cdot 6 \\\\  & =36.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy symmetry, the flux integral across the side ${C}_{3}$ must be the same. Therefore,\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot {n}_{1}\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{C}_{3}}F\\cdot {n}_{3}\\,\\text{d}s=36.\n$$\n\n- Next, we consider the side ${C}_{2}.$ We have that ${n}_{2}=j,$ and therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot {n}_{2}\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{{C}_{2}}(3x\\,i-2y\\,j)\\cdot j\\,\\text{d}s \\\\  & =-2{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}y\\,\\text{d}s.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is a line integral with respect to arc length, which we can evaluate in the usual way. Parameterizing the side ${C}_{2},$ we have\n\n$$\nr(t)=t\\,i+3j,\\,⇒\\,{r}^{′}(t)=i,\\,t\\in [-2,2].\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-2{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}y\\,\\text{d}s & =-2{\\int }_{-2}^{2}3\\cdot ||{r}^{′}(t)||\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-6{\\int }_{-2}^{2}\\text{d}t \\\\  & =(-6)\\cdot 4 \\\\  & =-24.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy symmetry, the flux integral across the side ${C}_{4}$ must be the same. Therefore,\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot {n}_{2}\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{C}_{4}}F\\cdot {n}_{4}\\,\\text{d}s=-24.\n$$\n\nFinally, then\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s=2\\cdot 36+2\\cdot (-24)=24.\n$$",
